-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- The Harmonic Algorithm — Interactive User Guide
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--
-- This file is a hands-on walkthrough. Scroll from top to bottom
-- and execute each block as you go. Every section introduces one
-- concept, builds on the last, and leaves bindings available for
-- the next section. Try the narrative prompts — change a value,
-- re-execute, listen.
--
-- ── MIDI OUTPUT ──
-- This system outputs MIDI on TidalCycles channels 1–16 (d01–d16).
-- Everything in this guide transmits on MIDI channel 1 (d01),
-- except the groove sections (channel 10, drums). Route channel 1
-- in your MIDI setup to a polyphonic sound — piano, Rhodes, pad,
-- polysynth. That's your instrument for the whole walkthrough.
--
-- ── THE GRAPH ──
-- Generation draws on a Neo4j graph of harmonic transitions
-- learned from 80+ composers (Yale Classical Archives Corpus).
-- Start it before you begin:
--
--     docker compose up -d neo4j
--
-- Confirm: curl -s http://localhost:7474 responds. If you can't
-- run Docker right now, `seek "none"` (Section 5) runs every
-- example on the built-in consonance fallback instead.
--
-- ── SETUP ──
-- Requires live/BootTidal.hs loaded with Harmonic.Lib. See the
-- BootTidal.hs file for all available helpers (ch, vel, oct, the
-- d01–d16 MIDI streams, hush/panic, and the drum patterns).
--
-- ── KNOWN EXCEPTIONS ──
-- Two areas are deliberately out of scope here:
--   * The Algorithmic Orchestration system (15-instrument virtual
--     orchestra) — depends on a specific hardware/MIDI rig. See
--     documents/ALGORITHMIC_ORCHESTRATION.md and ORCHESTRAL_CATALOGUE.tidal.
--   * Hardware-bound helpers in BootTidal.hs — Roland S-1 / P-6
--     CC maps, the Q-Link controller bridge, the LED display rig,
--     and the MPC kit program behind subKick. They're documented
--     in BootTidal.hs source comments; this guide stays on the
--     single piano channel plus generic drums.
--
-- ── SOURCE REFERENCES ──
-- Section headers include paths into src/ for every concept
-- introduced. Dig in whenever curious.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 0 — Setup & sound check
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--
-- GOAL: Confirm the three moving parts — MIDI, the library, and
-- the graph — before anything musical.

-- 1. MIDI: you should hear one middle C on the piano channel.
once $ note "0" # ch 01 # legato 0.9

-- 2. Library: the boot banner said "theHarmonicAlgorithm V3 boot
--    complete." If not, re-boot with live/BootTidal.hs.

-- 3. Graph: this generation should print a progression header
--    within a few seconds. If it errors with a connection
--    failure, Neo4j isn't up (docker compose up -d neo4j).
tempo = 90

start <- lead "C maj"

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

print s

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 1 — First generation
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Arranger.hs   (lead)
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder.hs          (seek, cue, len, entropy, gen)
--
-- GOAL: Understand the generation chain you just ran.
-- No sound yet — just the progression printed to the REPL.

-- `lead` builds a starting chord from a readable string.
-- Format: "<root> <quality> (<movement>)" — all optional.
start <- lead "C maj"

-- `seek` is the terminal that runs the whole modifier chain.
-- "*" aggregates the full composer corpus. The modifiers:
--   cue      — the starting state
--   len      — how many chords
--   entropy  — 0.0–1.0+: how far down the ranked candidate pool
--              the traversal is willing to reach
s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- Print the progression: header, harmonic context, chord grid.
print s

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Change "C maj" to "E min" or "Bb 7 (7)" and re-execute.
-- Change `len 4` to `len 8`. Try `entropy 0.2` (conventional,
-- cadence-hungry) vs `entropy 0.9` (adventurous, distant
-- modulations). Regenerate a few times at each — entropy is a
-- distribution, not a script.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 2 — Diagnostic output (gen' and gen'')
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder.hs             (gen', gen'')
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder/Diagnostics.hs (formatting)
--
-- GOAL: See what the algorithm is actually doing.
-- Swap `gen` for `gen'` to get a compact bar-by-bar diagnostic.

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen'

-- Sample gen' output (read the inline annotations):
--
--   Generation: C maj → 4 chords (entropy 0.5)
--   ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
--     1: C maj [starting state]
--
--     2: C → G  [14G/660F]  asc 7 → G maj  [graph] γ=3
--        Candidates: G maj | G dom7 | G min | E min | ...
--      …
--
-- Fields, left to right:
--   1, 2, 3        bar number
--   C → G          root movement (prior → posterior)
--   [NG/MF]        candidate pool: N graph, M fallback
--   asc 7          movement class (ascending 7 semitones)
--   G maj          selected chord at the posterior root
--   [graph/fallback] which pool the selection came from
--   γ=N            index chosen by gamma-entropy sampling
--                  (lower = safer, higher = more exploratory)
--   Candidates     top alternatives at the same root

-- gen'' adds verbose transform and advance traces — pitch-class
-- arithmetic, DB-stored vs computed functionality, every step of
-- the render pipeline. Try it once:
s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen''

-- ── OVERTONE ANNOTATION ──
-- When the context declares a tuning (Section 4), gen' annotates
-- each palette tone with its string/overtone sources:
--
--   {B: E2/G3, D: A3}
--
-- means B is producible as string E overtone 2 OR string G
-- overtone 3. Overtones cover the playable harmonic domain —
-- OT1 root, OT2 perfect fifth, OT3 major third. "/" separates
-- alternative sources; "+" joins several node positions on one
-- string.

-- ── WHEN TO USE ──
--   gen    — fast, just the progression
--   gen'   — understand selection, see the candidate pool
--   gen''  — debug rendering, see every computation step

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 3 — Play the progression (iK, form, launcher)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Form.hs      (at, iK)
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Bridge.hs    (arrange, rep)
--
-- GOAL: Turn a progression into sound.
-- This is the first audible generation. Make sure MIDI ch 1 is
-- routed.

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- A single-node form — constant kinetics & dynamics, one
-- progression. `at time kinetics dynamics progression` defines a
-- form node; with one node the signals are constant. (Section 13
-- builds real multi-node forms.)
form = [ at 0 1 1 s ]

-- `iK tempo formNodes chordSelectionPattern` bundles everything a
-- launcher needs. `rep s 1` auto-derives "one chord per bar".
k = iK tempo form (rep s 1)

-- A minimal launcher. `p01` is our pattern 01 — routed to d01
-- (MIDI channel 1). `f` is a transformation (id here), `k` the
-- IK context, `d` a dynamics scalar. This launcher is reused
-- throughout the guide.
p01 f k d = d01 $ do
  let o = ch 01
  f
    $ stack [silence
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[0 1 2 3]/4"
      ] # o |* vel 0.8 |= legato 0.95
    ] |* vel d

-- Launch it.
do
  let k = iK tempo form (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

-- Anatomy of the arrange call:
--   (0, 1)       kinetics range — play when kinetics is inside
--   k            the IK context
--   (-9, 9)      register bounds (semitones around centre)
--   T            progression layer (T = triads; S/M in Section 19)
--   flow         voicing function (Section 7)
--   (overlapF 0) progression modifier (Section 9)
--   ["~", pat]   patterns of voicing DEGREES (0 = lowest voice)

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Change the pattern to "[0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7]/8" — twice the
-- density. Try "[0 2 1 3]/4" for a different contour. One cycle
-- is one beat; a bar-length pattern carries /4.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 4 — Shape the generation (HarmonicContext)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder/Types.hs   (HarmonicContext, modifiers)
-- src/Harmonic/Rules/Constraints/Filter.hs  (filter string syntax)
--
-- GOAL: Constrain what the generator can choose from.
-- Modifiers compose right-to-left with `$`. Comment lines out to
-- fall back to defaults. Everything builds on `hContext`, the
-- chromatic default.
--
--   hcOvertones "E A D G"  — pitch palette: each named fundamental
--                            expands to {root, P5, M3} — its
--                            playable overtone set
--   hcKey       "0#"       — key signature (0#=C, 1#=G, 2b=Bb)
--   hcRoots     "C E G"    — allowed bass notes
--   hcPedal     "C"        — required tone in every chord
--   hcPedal     "C G?"     — C required, G preferred
--   consonant              — drift toward calmer harmony
--   dissonant              — drift toward increasing tension
--   invSkip N              — min non-inversions between inversions

-- Start with everything chromatic
ctx0 = hContext

-- Layer a key filter
ctxKey = hcKey "0#" $ hContext

-- A rich, composable context (the kind you'd use in performance)
ctx = invSkip 1
    $ consonant
    $ hcPedal "C?"
    $ hcKey "0#"
    $ hContext

-- Regenerate with the new context — `tonal` passes it in
s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen'

-- Relaunch with the same launcher
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

-- ── FILTER STRING TRICKS ──
--   "*"            wildcard (match everything)
--   "-Bb'"         subtract a specific pitch (prime = exact pitch)
--   "E A D G"      overtone palettes of each fundamental
--   "E' A'"        prime notation = only the exact pitches
--   "key"          mirror the key filter (in hcRoots)
--   "rise"/"fall"  force stepwise ascending/descending bass

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Swap `consonant` for `dissonant` and re-run — the tension
-- rises bar over bar. Remove `hcPedal "C?"` — the held C lets
-- go. Add `hcOvertones "E A D G"` and watch gen' annotate every
-- palette tone with its string/overtone sources.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 5 — The composer graph
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Evaluation/Database/Query.hs  (composer weight parsing)
--
-- GOAL: Channel the harmonic style of individual composers, or
-- weighted blends of several.
--
-- The `seek` string selects the corpus view:
--   "*"                    aggregate across all composers
--   "bach"                 one composer's transition edges
--   "bach:25 debussy:75"   weighted blend — the gen' header shows
--                          a portmanteau ("Bachdebussy")
--   "none"                 OFFLINE — bypass the graph entirely and
--                          use the consonance fallback. This is the
--                          escape hatch when Docker isn't available;
--                          every example in this guide still runs.
-- Names are case-insensitive.

-- Single composer
s <- seek "bach" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen'

-- Different composer — compare the harmonic character
s <- seek "debussy" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen'

-- Weighted blend
s <- seek "bach:25 debussy:75" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen'

-- Offline fallback — no graph, still musical
s <- seek "none" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen'

-- Play any of them:
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Compare Bach's cadential pull with Debussy's modal inflections
-- on the same seed and context. Stack a composer you know with
-- one you don't — hear what they share. Then run the same chain
-- with "none" and hear what the corpus was adding.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 6 — Rank and select (attempt, viability)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder.hs           (attempt, viability)
-- src/Harmonic/Evaluation/Scoring/Progression.hs (the scoring)
--
-- GOAL: Generate several candidate progressions and keep the
-- best, instead of accepting the first.
--
--   attempt N K  — up to K attempts, stop early once N viable
--                  ones are collected, return the single highest
--                  scoring progression
--   viability T  — the score floor an attempt must clear to count
--                  as viable (default 0.5; 0.0 = structural only)
--
-- Scoring ranks by cadence-favourability (dominant weight), plus
-- resolution, voice-leading and motion terms. `attempt` wraps any
-- generation chain — gen, genFrom, genP — uniformly.

-- Best of up to 12, stop at 3 viable:
s <- seek "*" $ attempt 3 12 $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.6 $ gen

-- With Verbose verbosity, a full scoreboard prints after the
-- run — one row per attempt with per-term scores, a viability
-- marker, the truncated chord sequence, and "← PICK" on the
-- winner. Watch what "better" means to the evaluator:
s <- seek "*" $ attempt 3 12 $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.6 $ gen''

-- Stricter search / structural-only:
s <- seek "*" $ viability 0.7 $ attempt 5 24 $ cue start $ len 8 $ entropy 0.6 $ gen
s <- seek "*" $ viability 0.0 $ attempt 3 12 $ cue start $ len 8 $ entropy 0.6 $ gen

do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Raise entropy to 0.9 and watch the scoreboard: more attempts
-- fail viability, and the spread between best and worst widens.
-- `attempt 1 1` is a no-op — the scoreboard stays quiet.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 7 — Voicing strategies
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Arranger.hs  (flow, grid, lite, root)
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Groove.hs    (fund)
--
-- GOAL: Change the vertical texture without changing the harmony.
-- Same progression, same pattern, swap the voicing function.
--
--   flow  — smoothest voice leading (cyclic DP, any inversion)
--   grid  — root locked in bass, smooth upper voices
--   lite  — raw intervals, no voice leading
--   root  — root note only (bass line)
--   fund  — harmonic fundamental always (sub bass, kick)

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- Full voicing with smooth flow
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0 1 2 3]/4"] # ch 01 |* vel 0.8)
    ]

hush

-- Now grid — listen to the bass locking down
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T grid (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0 1 2 3]/4"] # ch 01 |* vel 0.8)
    ]

hush

-- Just the root line
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T root (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0]/1"] # ch 01 |- oct 2 |* vel 0.9)
    ]

hush

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Stack two arrange calls with different voicings in a single
-- launcher (flow for chords, root for bass) — Section 17 builds
-- a full four-voice texture this way.


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 8 — Chord selection (rep vs warp)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Bridge.hs   (warp, rep)
--
-- GOAL: Control which chord plays when.
--
--   rep s N    — one chord per bar × N (auto-derived length)
--   warp "..." — explicit 1-indexed selection in mininotation,
--                divisor = physical bars over which to step

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- Default: one chord per bar
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- Explicit selection: chords 1, 2, 3, 4 over 4 bars (identical)
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (warp "[1 2 3 4]/4")
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- AABA-like cycle across 4 bars
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (warp "[1 1 2 1]/4")
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- TidalCycles operators work inside warp
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (warp "[1 <2 3> 1 4]/4")
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- warp "[1 2 3 4 3 2 1 4]/4"  — 8 chord events over 4 bars
-- warp "[1 2 3 4]/8"           — slower: 4 chords over 8 bars
-- warp "[1(3,8) 2]/4"          — euclidean rhythm on chord 1
-- warp "[1 . [2 3]]/4"         — nested rhythm
-- Indices wrap modulo the progression length.


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 9 — arrange vs arrange' (across vs within)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Bridge.hs  (arrange, arrange')
--
-- GOAL: Understand how patterns interact with progression
-- changes.
--
--   arrange   — pattern runs at its own speed; pitches map to
--               whichever chord is active at each onset
--   arrange'  — pattern is redistributed within each chord slot;
--               every chord gets the full pattern, compressed
--
-- Notes that cross a harmony boundary sustain naturally (a new
-- onset is only generated within the chord that started the
-- note). `overlapF N` merges pitches from N bars ahead into each
-- chord's pitch set for even smoother legato; `progOverlapB` /
-- `progOverlap` (Section 10) are the backward and bidirectional
-- forms at the progression level.

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- arrange: pattern flows across the whole progression
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 1) ["~"
        , "[0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7]/2"
      ] # ch 01 |* vel 0.8)
    ]

hush

-- arrange' (squeeze): the same pattern repeats in every slot
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange' (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7]/2"
      ] # ch 01 |* vel 0.8)
    ]

hush

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- arrange feels like a melody flowing over changing chords.
-- arrange' feels like an arpeggiator locked to each chord.
-- Change `overlapF 0` to `overlapF 2` — neighbouring chords'
-- pitches merge into the current set (smoother legato).


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 10 — Progression manipulation (arranger suite)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Arranger.hs
--
-- GOAL: Transform harmony in real time with pure functions.
-- All return a new progression. Chain them freely.
--
--   rotate N          — rotate by N bars
--   excerpt start end — extract a range (1-indexed, inclusive)
--   transposeP N      — transpose by N semitones
--   reverse           — reverse the order
--   fuse [a, b, c]    — concatenate progressions
--   fuse2 a b         — binary fuse
--   interleave a b    — alternating chords
--   expandP N         — each chord repeated N times
--   insert cs N p     — replace bar N with a CadenceState
--   switch M N        — swap bars M and N
--   clone M N         — copy bar M to position N
--   extract N p       — pull out a single CadenceState
--   progOverlapF N    — merge pitches from N bars ahead
--   progOverlapB N    — merge pitches from N bars behind
--   progOverlap N     — merge in both directions

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- Rotate by 2 bars (progression starts from chord 3)
do
  let s' = rotate 2 s
      k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s'] (rep s' 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- Extract the first 4 bars and play them
do
  let s' = excerpt 1 4 s
      k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s'] (rep s' 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- Transpose up a fourth (5 semitones)
do
  let s' = transposeP 5 s
      k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s'] (rep s' 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- Fuse the original with its reverse (palindromic motion)
do
  let s' = fuse2 s (Harmonic.Interface.Tidal.Arranger.reverse s)
      k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s'] (rep s' 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- Interleave with a transposed copy (harmonic alternation)
do
  let s' = interleave s (transposeP 5 s)
      k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s'] (rep s' 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Chain transformations: `transposeP 7 $ rotate 3 $ s`
-- `expandP 2 s` slows the harmonic rhythm (each chord twice)
-- `progOverlapF 1 s` merges each chord with its neighbour
-- (natural sustain across transitions)
-- `genGrid` in a seek chain skips traversal entirely and prints
-- the candidate grid — a raw view of the R component.


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 11 — Explicit composition (non-generation path)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Arranger.hs  (fromChords, prog)
-- src/Harmonic/Rules/Types/Pitch.hs         (notesToPCs, NoteName)
--
-- GOAL: Build progressions by hand when you have specific
-- changes in mind. Use generation when you want suggestions.
-- Generated and explicit progressions are interchangeable —
-- every voicing, arrangement, form and manipulation applies to
-- both.

-- Pitch-class list construction — explicit intervals per bar
sExplicit = fromChords
  [ [0, 4, 7]    -- C maj
  , [5, 9, 0]    -- F maj
  , [7, 11, 2]   -- G maj
  , [9, 0, 4]    -- A min
  ]

-- Note-name syntax — more readable. `notesToPCs` maps NoteName
-- lists to pitch classes. Accidentals: prime = sharp (C' is C#,
-- F' is F#), and flat names exist too (Db, Eb, …).
sNamed = prog (notesToPCs <$>
  [ [C, E, G]    -- C maj
  , [F, A, C]    -- F maj
  , [G, B, D]    -- G maj
  , [A, C, E]    -- A min
  ])

-- Full CadenceState construction with explicit root movement
sStates = fromCadenceStates
  [ initCadenceState 0 "C" [0, 4, 7]   -- C maj, unison start
  , initCadenceState 5 "F" [0, 4, 7]   -- F maj, asc 5
  , initCadenceState 2 "G" [0, 4, 7]   -- G maj, asc 2
  , initCadenceState 2 "A" [0, 3, 7]   -- A min, asc 2
  ]

-- Play the note-named version
do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 sNamed] (rep sNamed 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Build your own 8-bar progression with note names. Apply a
-- Section 10 manipulation: `transposeP 2 sNamed`. Interleave it
-- with a generated progression — hand and machine in one form.


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 12 — Regenerate in place (genFrom)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder.hs  (genFrom, genFrom', genFrom'')
--
-- GOAL: Keep the progression you like; regenerate only the bars
-- you don't. Surgery, not do-over.
--
--   genFrom s a b — regenerate bars a..b of s (1-indexed,
--                   wrap-aware: on a 5-bar progression,
--                   genFrom s 4 2 walks 4,5,1,2 and keeps 3).
--
-- The cue is auto-inferred from the bar before `a` so the new
-- material connects; override with `cue`. The printed grid is
-- always the FULL spliced progression — you see the source with
-- the new bars in place. Composes with the whole modifier chain,
-- including `attempt`.

s8 <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- The piece is right except bars 3–4:
s8' <- seek "*" $ entropy 0.5 $ genFrom s8 3 4

print s8
print s8'      -- diff bars 3–4 by eye

-- With rank-and-select on the patch:
s8'' <- seek "*" $ attempt 3 12 $ entropy 0.5 $ genFrom s8 3 4

-- Diagnostic variants mirror gen:
--   genFrom'   — standard per-bar trace
--   genFrom''  — verbose + scoreboard under attempt

do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s8''] (rep s8'' 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    ]

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Regenerate the FINAL bars (genFrom s8 7 8) — the seam back to
-- bar 1 is handled (the progression cycles). Then a wrapping
-- range (genFrom s8 7 2). A/B each patch against the original.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 13 — Kinetics form (programmed arc)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Form.hs
--
-- GOAL: Program a macro-level arc in wall-clock time.
-- A form is a list of `at time kinetics dynamics progression`
-- nodes. Between nodes the kinetics and dynamics interpolate
-- linearly; the progression switches discretely at each node.
--
-- The kinetics signal (0–1) drives range-gating on `arrange`:
-- patterns with kinetics range `(lo, hi)` only play when the
-- current value is inside that window. The dynamics signal (0–1)
-- drives the amplitude envelope.

-- Two progressions — one calm, one adventurous
sA <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal (consonant $ hcKey "0#" $ hContext) $ len 4 $ entropy 0.3 $ gen
sB <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal (dissonant $ hcKey "0#" $ hContext) $ len 4 $ entropy 0.85 $ gen

-- A 60-second arc with progression switching at the peak
arcForm =
  [ at   0   0.0  0.0  sA   -- silence
  , at   5   0.3  0.4  sA   -- fade in on calm
  , at  20   0.5  0.6  sA   -- calm sustains
  , at  30   0.8  0.8  sB   -- switch to adventurous at the rise
  , at  40   1.0  1.0  sB   -- peak
  , at  50   0.5  0.65 sA   -- return to calm
  , at  60   0.0  0.0  sA   -- fade out
  , at  70   0.0  0.0  sA   -- silence gap
  ]

-- A launcher with two patterns gated at different ranges
p01arc f k d = d01 $ do
  let o = ch 01
  f
    $ stack [silence
      -- Chord pad throughout
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[0 1 2 3]/4"
      ] # o |* vel 0.75
      -- Upper line only in the high-kinetics middle section
      , arrange (0.7, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[0 2 4 7 4 2]/4"
      ] # o |+ oct 1 |* vel 0.6
    ] |* vel d

-- Launch the arc
do
  let k = iK tempo arcForm (rep sA 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01arc id k 0.85
    ]

-- ── SNAP TRANSITIONS & BAR-BASED NODES ──
-- `at` is the seconds/smooth constructor. Three siblings vary
-- two orthogonal axes — time unit and transition:
--
--   at   seconds   smooth (ramp between nodes)
--   at'  seconds   snap   (hold, then jump on the exact time)
--   rh   bars      smooth (rh = "rehearsal mark"; bar = 4 beats)
--   rh'  bars      snap
--
-- Snap suits hard cuts; bars align nodes to the metre. Mix them:
cutForm =
  [ rh   0   0.0  0.0  sA   -- bar 0: silence
  , rh   4   0.5  0.6  sA   -- bar 4: smooth fade-in
  , rh'  8   1.0  1.0  sB   -- bar 8: SNAP to full
  , rh  16   0.4  0.5  sB   -- bar 16: ramp back down
  , at' 40   0.0  0.0  sA   -- 40s: snap to silence (mixing units is fine)
  ]

do
  let k = iK tempo cutForm (rep sA 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01arc id k 0.85
    ]

-- ── FORM TEMPLATES ──
-- Reusable arc shapes, scaled to taste (multiply the node times):
--
-- Spectral narrative (~7m24s at these times; the classic slow arc):
narrative sA sB =
  [ at    0  0.0  0.0  sA
  , at   20  0.2  0.3  sA
  , at  100  0.4  0.5  sA
  , at  180  0.6  0.7  sA
  , at  260  0.8  0.85 sB
  , at  320  1.0  1.0  sB
  , at  360  0.7  0.8  sB
  , at  400  0.4  0.5  sA
  , at  440  0.1  0.2  sA
  , at  454  0.0  0.0  sA
  ]
--
-- Pop form (~3m at 112 BPM; verse-chorus-bridge in bars):
popForm sV sC sBr =
  [ rh   0  0.3  0.5  sV    -- verse
  , rh  16  0.7  0.8  sC    -- chorus
  , rh  32  0.35 0.5  sV    -- verse 2
  , rh  48  0.75 0.85 sC    -- chorus 2
  , rh  64  0.5  0.6  sBr   -- bridge
  , rh  80  0.9  1.0  sC    -- final chorus
  , rh  96  0.0  0.0  sV    -- out
  ]

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Stretch arcForm to 5 minutes or compress to 20s. Change the
-- upper line's gate `(0.7, 1)` to `(0, 0.3)` — it becomes the
-- lead-in instead. Flip `rh' 8` to `rh 8` — the hard cut
-- becomes a ramp.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 14 — Live kinetics & gating (lK, ki, slate)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Form.hs  (lK, ki, slate, withForm)
--
-- GOAL: Drive kinetics from a live signal instead of a keyframed
-- form — the arc becomes a control you perform.
--
--   lK kinSig dynSig s chordSel — build an IK from any two
--     `Pattern Double` signals (0–1). Everything downstream
--     (range gating, dynamics) behaves exactly as with iK.
--
-- Any signal works: an LFO, a random walk, a MIDI CC. (The
-- Q-Link controller bridge in BootTidal.hs — qlink1..4, xyX/xyY,
-- exP — supplies hardware CC signals on this interface; see the
-- source comments. The LED display feed `display k` is likewise
-- hardware-specific; both are pointers, not walkthrough scope.)

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- A slow LFO performing the arc for you:
do
  let kin = slow 64 $ lfo tri 0 1        -- 64-beat triangle sweep
      k   = lK kin kin s (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01arc id k 0.85
    ]

hush

-- ── GATING HELPERS ──
-- The same range-gating that arrange applies is available on ANY
-- pattern:
--
--   ki (lo, hi) k pat      — mask pat to the kinetics window
--   slate (lo, hi) k pats  — stack pats, gated as one
--
-- slate builds kinetics-layered drum kits and texture stacks
-- without arrange:

do
  let kin = slow 32 $ lfo tri 0 1
      k   = lK kin kin s (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.85
    , d10 $ stack
      [ slate (0.2, 1)   k [ kick "[1 0 0 0]/4" ]        -- kick from 0.2
      , slate (0.5, 1)   k [ hhcl "[0 1 0 1]/4" ]        -- hats join at 0.5
      , slate (0.8, 1)   k [ snare "[0 0 1 0]/4" ]       -- snare only at the top
      ]
    ]

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Replace the LFO with `slow 16 $ lfo sine 0 1`, or a random
-- walk (`segment 8 $ perlin`). `withForm k f` gives f the active
-- progression reactively — for building custom pattern functions
-- that follow form switches.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 15 — Groove (subKick)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Groove.hs
--
-- GOAL: Kick and sub bass that follow the harmonic root.
-- subKick combines a kick pattern, a sustained sub, and an
-- MPC-style CC64 sustain mechanism. It locks to the root of
-- whichever chord is active at each onset.
--
-- `subKick d k voiceFunc (maxDur, subOn, subOff, kickPat)`
-- routes to MIDI channel 10. (A complementary MPC kit program
-- renders the full-range sub+kick; any drum sound on ch 10
-- works for the walkthrough.)

subk f k d = p "subKick"
  $ f
    $ subKick d k root ( 1/4
      , "[1(3,8) . ~]"    -- sub bass on: euclidean 3-of-8 with rest
      , "[0 1 0 0]"       -- sub bass off: control
      , "1*4"              -- kick: four on the floor
    )

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal ctx $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01 id k 0.9
    , subk id k 0.8
    ]

hush

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Change the kick to "[1 0 0 1]" (pushed) or a clave
-- (`son32` gates work anywhere: kick son32). Swap `root` for
-- `fund` — the sub follows the harmonic fundamental instead of
-- the chord root (inversion-proof low end).


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 16 — Walking lines (walk / lineHarmony)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/LineHarmony.hs
-- src/Harmonic/Traversal/WalkingBass.hs
--
-- GOAL: A walking bassline synthesised from the progression —
-- consonant anchors on the strong beats (root on 1; P5/root/3rds
-- on 3), weighted connectors on 2 and 4 preferring sandwich
-- motion and approach tones, with a soft direction-persistence
-- bias on the beat-1 contour. The line is fixed to the
-- double-bass register (E1–C3) and derives its character from
-- the harmony (progression-level consonance scales strictness).
--
-- The walk follows the PERFORMED bar order: warp / rep
-- selections are resolved at eval time, repeated bars walk as
-- neighbours (root–fifth alternation), and approach tones aim
-- at the bar that actually comes next. Non-periodic selections
-- (`?`, euclidean, sub-bar values) fall back to stored order
-- with a printed notice.
--
--   lineHarmony d k voiceFn patterns
--     d        — dynamics: applied once inside lineHarmony (do
--                NOT also |* vel d outside — that squares it).
--                Dynamics also STEER the walk: bars quieter than
--                the piece's own mean bias the register arc up,
--                louder bars down, and a sudden drop (0.25+
--                between bars) resets the line to its lowest
--                note. Only eval-time-sampleable dynamics count:
--                the d argument and form-node dynamics — live
--                control signals and downstream |* vel are
--                walk-neutral.
--     voiceFn  — beat-1 anchor voicing: `root` or `fund`
--     patterns — 1-indexed BEAT POSITIONS (1 2 3 4), not degrees:
--                which beats of each bar the line sounds on.
--                NOTE kinPick windowing: the list length N
--                partitions the kinetics signal into N windows
--                and plays ONLY the pattern whose window holds
--                the current signal — a leading "~" makes the
--                low-kinetics half silent.

walk f k d = p "lineHarmony"
  $ f
    $ lineHarmony d k root [ "~"
      , "[1 2 3 4]/4"          -- full quarters
    ] # ch 01 # legato 0.95

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal (hcKey "0#" $ hContext) $ len 8 $ entropy 0.4 $ gen

do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0,1,2]/1"] # ch 01 |+ oct 1 |* vel 0.4 |= legato 3.6)
    , walk id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- Sparser walks — the beat-position patterns compose like any
-- Tidal pattern:
--   ["~", "[1 3]/4"]        — two-feel (beats 1 and 3)
--   ["~", "[1 2 ~ 4]/4"]    — skip beat 3
--   ["~", "[1 [2 2] 3 4]/4"] — an eighth-note fill on beat 2
--
-- For octatripentatonic progressions (Section 19) the line's
-- connector pool automatically reweights toward the 5-PC strata
-- set — the walk speaks the same dialect as the harmony.
--
-- For jazz progressions (Section 21) each bar is read through its
-- chord symbol's bass vocabulary: the fifth a 13th chord omits is
-- restored, an altered dominant's #5 stands in for the natural 5,
-- and notated colours (b9 #9 #11 b13) never land on strong beats —
-- they stay available as weak-beat tension. Nothing to switch on;
-- the walk follows the progression's family.

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Mute the pad and listen to the line alone — the strong beats
-- carry the changes by themselves. Swap `root` for `fund` on an
-- inversion-heavy progression and hear beat 1 stabilise.


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 17 — Voice lines & the instrument paradigm
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/Orchestra.hs
-- live/ORCHESTRAL_CATALOGUE.tidal     (instrument catalogue)
-- documents/ALGORITHMIC_ORCHESTRATION.md        (full orchestration system)
--
-- GOAL: Address stacked voices as independent patterns — each
-- with its own kinetics range, voicing function and register.
--
-- The core idea: stack several `arrange` calls in one launcher,
-- each reading a different voicing degree in a different octave.

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal (hcKey "0#" $ hContext) $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

p01satb f k d = d01 $ do
  let o = ch 01
  f
    $ stack [silence
      -- Soprano — top voice
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[3]/1"
      ] # o |+ oct 1 |* vel 0.7
      -- Alto — upper middle
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[2]/1"
      ] # o |* vel 0.6
      -- Tenor — lower middle
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[1]/1"
      ] # o |* vel 0.6
      -- Bass
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T root (overlapF 0) ["~"
        , "[0]/1"
      ] # o |- oct 1 |* vel 0.8
    ] |* vel d

do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01satb id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- ── DIVISI ──
-- When a voice splits into desks, each desk is quieter than the
-- undivided line. Divisi handles the split and the equal-power
-- (1/sqrt n) compensation. Each voice carries three tiers in one
-- voiceLines record — base, ', '' — all ordinary degree
-- patterns; unset tiers default one/two degrees up.
p01div f k d = d01 $ do
  let o  = ch 01
      vl = voiceLines {_vl = "~"
        , soprano = "[3 2 1 0]/4"    -- desk 1
        , soprano' = "[5 4 3 2]/4"   -- desk 2 (a third up)
        }
  f
    $ stack [silence
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", vlGet Soprano  vl] # o # divisi2
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", vlGet Soprano' vl] # o # divisi2 |+ oct 1
    ] |* vel d

do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01div id k 0.9
    ]

hush

-- With the orchestral instrument functions the same split is one
-- line — `divisi 2 violin1 T (0, 1) k vl grid Soprano` — and
-- primed voices (Soprano', Bass8vb') pick desks manually. The
-- full system (16 instruments — 14 pitched, 2 unpitched — articulations, section blocks,
-- timbral blends) lives in documents/ALGORITHMIC_ORCHESTRATION.md; it
-- depends on a specific MIDI rig, so this guide stops at the
-- paradigm.


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 18 — Motifs (motivic development)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- live/BootTidal.hs  (>:<, mirror, clave library)
--
-- GOAL: Name the recurring material a piece is built from, and
-- develop it with the classic tools instead of retyping strings.
--
-- Motifs are plain patterns — a `Pattern Bool` rhythm and a
-- `Pattern Int` contour (voicing degrees, realised against the
-- harmony by `arrange`, so a contour tracks the chords).

s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal (hcKey "0#" $ hContext) $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen

-- One statement binds all three names; `motif = rhythm >:< contour`
-- (>:< gates the contour with the rhythm's onsets). Primed tiers
-- (rhythm'/contour''/…) fall back to BootTidal defaults.
(rhythm, contour, motif) =
  ( son32                 -- rhythm gate  (a son clave, from BootTidal)
  , "[3 2 1 0]/4"         -- contour      (a descending figure)
  , rhythm >:< contour
  ) :: (Pattern Bool, Pattern Int, Pattern Int)

-- The classic developments are just Tidal, plus mirror / >:< :
--   retro contour       retrograde      mirror 3 contour  inversion (axis 3)
--   slow 2 motif        augmentation    fast 2 motif      diminution
--   contour |+ 2        transposition   "<0 1>" <~ rhythm rotation
--
-- ── SCOPE: cycle vs bar ──
-- These transforms work on the CYCLE. One cycle is one beat here, so a
-- bar-length figure carries `/4` and spans four cycles — and Tidal's `rev`
-- then reverses each beat on its own rather than retrograding the phrase.
-- `retro` is provided for this (BootTidal, beside `mirror`):
--
--   rev    contour   -- each beat reversed; phrase order unchanged
--   retro  contour   -- true phrase retrograde  (retro . retro = id)
--   retroN 8 motif   -- retrograde over n cycles (here, two bars)
--
-- The same caveat applies to rotation. `~>` shifts in cycles, so shifting a
-- figure by a multiple of its own period is a silent no-op — rotating
-- `"[3 2 0 1]*4"` (period 1/4 cycle) by 1/4 lands back on itself. Rotate
-- bar-scoped material, or shift by a fraction of the period:
--
--   0.5 ~> contour                      -- canon at the half-beat
--
-- Check a development before trusting it. Note that `queryArc` returns
-- events in query order, NOT time order, so inspect with the start time
-- attached or sort first — otherwise a working `rev` looks like a no-op:
--
--   [ (wholeStart e, value e) | e <- queryArc (rev contour) (Arc 0 1) ]

p01mot f k d = d01 $ do
  let o = ch 01
  f
    $ stack [silence
      -- the contour as an upper voice
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", contour] # o |+ oct 1 |* vel 0.7
      -- its inversion, underneath
      , arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", mirror 3 contour] # o |* vel 0.5
      -- the son clave gating the root
      , struct rhythm $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T root (overlapF 0) ["~", "0"] # o |- oct 1 |* vel 0.8
    ] |* vel d

do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 s] (rep s 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , p01mot id k 0.9
    ]

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Re-execute the panel with `contour = "[0 1 2 3]/4"` or
-- `rhythm = bossa32` and hear p01mot reprogram in one step.
-- Swap `mirror 3` for `rev`. Claves in BootTidal: son32,
-- rumba32, bossa32, bellpat32 (+ 23 rotations: son23 etc).

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 19 — The three layers (T/S/M) & genP
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- documents/OCTATRIPENTATONICS.md                       (the framework reference)
-- src/Harmonic/Rules/Types/Scale.hs           (strata vocabulary)
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder/Strata.hs    (the walk)
-- src/Harmonic/Interface/Tidal/OctatripentatonicT.hs (genPReport)
--
-- GOAL: Every progression carries three bar-aligned layers:
--
--   T  triad layer   3 PCs   the chords
--   S  strata layer  5 PCs   a pentatonic per bar
--   M  mode layer    7 PCs   a diatonic mode per bar
--
-- The `arrange` layer argument selects which one patterns read.
-- Plain `gen` fills all three with the triad; the strata-first
-- generator `genP` populates them for real, by walking eleven
-- canonical pentatonic STRATA (I–XI) grouped into twelve curated
-- TRISTRATA whose pairwise unions are diatonic modes. Full
-- theory: documents/OCTATRIPENTATONICS.md.
--
--   genP <label>  — seed the walk at a strata; 33 aliases:
--   genI..genXI (+ ' standard / '' verbose diagnostics)

start <- lead "E min"

-- Standard verbosity prints per-bar triad + strata (5 PCs) +
-- mode (7 PCs) + tristrata identity — the full anatomy:
pc19 <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ genVI'

-- Hear the three densities of the SAME harmony — swap the layer:
p19 layer = do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 pc19] (rep pc19 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) layer flow (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0 1 2 3]/4"] # ch 01 |* vel 0.7 |= legato 1.2)
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T root (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0]/1"] # ch 01 |- oct 2 |* vel 0.7 |= legato 3.6)
    ]

p19 T    -- triads: 3 tones cycle
p19 S    -- strata: 5-tone pentatonic per bar
p19 M    -- mode: 7-tone diatonic per bar

hush

-- ── STEERING THE WALK ──

-- Lock to one tristrata (#5 = IV-VI-X) — three strata, one
-- eight-tone world:
pcLock <- seek "*" $ cue start $ tonal (hcTristrata "5" hContext) $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ genVI'

-- Per-bar RELATIVE position (1..3) inside the active tristrata
-- (length auto-set from the list):
pcRel <- seek "*" $ cue start $ relStrata "1 1 2 2 3 3" $ genVI'

-- Per-bar ABSOLUTE strata labels:
pcAbs <- seek "*" $ cue start $ absStrata "I V X" $ genI'

-- Provenance + triads in one report:
genPReport (pure pcAbs)

-- ── CONTINUITY BOOSTS ──
-- The walk prefers staying put. Three multipliers bias it
-- (defaults 0.90 / 0.80 / 0.70; 1.0 disables that bias):
--   sameBoost — same strata as the previous bar
--   flipBoost — same strata as two bars ago (alternation)
--   triBoost  — same tristrata as the previous bar
pcFree <- seek "*" $ cue start $ sameBoost 1.0 $ triBoost 1.0 $ len 8 $ genVI'

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- `p19 S` under a soloist (you) is pentatonic practice with
-- moving ground. `attempt 3 12 $ ... $ genVI''` ranks whole
-- walks. The walking bass (Section 16) reweights automatically
-- over genP progressions — stack `walk` under `p19 M`.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 20 — Four-note generation (genE)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder.hs        (quad, genE family)
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder/Core.hs   (fuseState)
--
-- GOAL: Seventh-chord density with the same corpus-trained walk.
-- A third generator family alongside gen and genP: each step
-- picks a triad exactly as gen does, then FUSES one more tone
-- from the R-valid palette (most-consonant-first ranking, same
-- entropy dial). The walk continues from the fused chord's most
-- consonant embedded triad, so the added tone can reinterpret
-- the harmony — while every graph key stays corpus-shaped and
-- generation stays online.

start <- lead "C maj"

-- A triad cue is fused once — output is uniformly 4-note.
-- Watch the new trace line under each bar's Candidates:
--   fused: +Ab → F m7  [rank 2/9]
-- (which tone was added, the fused name, where the draw landed
-- in the consonant-first ranking).
pc20 <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genE'

-- Play it. Degree 3 in the pattern is now a real fourth chord
-- tone, not the octave wrap.
p20 = do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 pc20] (rep pc20 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0 1 2 3]/4"] # ch 01 |* vel 0.7 |= legato 1.2)
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T root (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0]/1"] # ch 01 |- oct 2 |* vel 0.7 |= legato 3.6)
    ]

p20

-- ── EXPLICIT 4-NOTE CUES: lead' ──
-- lead' takes note names directly. First note = root/bass; typed
-- accidentals drive the spelling (Eb → flat, D# → sharp);
-- optional (N) fixes the approach movement, like lead.
start' <- lead' "Eb Gb Bb Db"        -- Eb m7, used as-is (no fuse)
pc20' <- seek "*" $ cue start' $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genE'

-- ── COMPOSES LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE ──
-- quad is the underlying modifier (genE = quad gen):
pc20'' <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 8 $ attempt 3 12 $ entropy 0.4 $ genE''

-- Regeneration is family-aware: a uniform 4-note source regenerates
-- 4-note bars automatically (families never mix; quad is inferred):
pc20r <- seek "*" $ entropy 0.3 $ genFrom pc20 3 5

-- R adherence is structural: the added tone always comes from
-- the active palette (tonal / hcKey), the bass never moves, and
-- consonant/dissonant drift governs BOTH the triad skeleton and
-- the fused surface:
pc20k <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 8 $ tonal (hcKey "0#" $ consonant hContext) $ genE'

-- One boundary: genE and genP never mix — strata progressions
-- stay 3-5-7 by design. quad on a strata source fails fast.

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Generate the same cue with gen' then genE' and compare grids:
-- same walk character, thicker sonorities. Then play pc20 with
-- lite instead of flow and listen to the register difference.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 21 — Jazz generation (genJ)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- src/Harmonic/Framework/Builder/JazzGen.hs   (the jazz walk)
-- src/Harmonic/Rules/Import/Jazz.hs           (parser + namer)
--
-- GOAL: leadsheet changes — m7, 7#9, 13b9#11, slash chords —
-- walked with the same modifier chain, trained on the
-- Jazz-Chord-Progressions-Corpus (Bunks et al., ISMIR 2023;
-- 2,614 tunes). The jazz graph (:Change) lives beside the
-- classical one; chords come out at corpus arity (3-6 tones)
-- and there is deliberately NO consonance fallback — the
-- corpus is rich enough that the graph IS the pool.

-- Cue with leadJ: leadsheet symbols in their own quality
-- namespace (lead/lead' and the triadic tables are untouched).
-- A notated slash bass is honoured exactly: unioned into the
-- set and made the anchor.
jstart <- leadJ "Cm7"                -- corpus workhorse
jslash <- leadJ "Dm7/G"              -- G anchor, full Dm7 above (9sus4 set)

pc21 <- seek "*" $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genJ'

-- ── ONE SEEK SPEC, BOTH CORPORA ──
-- Jazz names blend by corpus weights (substring-matched:
-- "monk" finds theloniousmonk and the co-credits). A CLASSICAL
-- name steers instead: each step, the chord's most consonant
-- embedded triad asks the classical graph what that composer
-- would do next, and jazz candidates containing a recommended
-- triad get boosted. steer dials the strength.
pc21b <- seek "theloniousmonk:60 johncoltrane:40" $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ genJ'
pc21s <- seek "debussy" $ steer 8 $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genJ'
pc21m <- seek "monk debussy" $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genJ'
-- (seek "none" is refused — the jazz graph has no offline mode.)

-- ── THE USUAL MODIFIERS ──
-- tonal runs the same R filters over jazz candidates (key of C
-- pins the walk to diatonic corpus vocabulary — Am7, Dm7,
-- G7sus4, CM7...); genFrom regenerates jazz-natively; attempt
-- ranks against the jazz graph's own cadence statistics.
pc21k <- seek "*" $ cue jstart $ tonal (hcKey "0#" hContext) $ len 8 $ entropy 0.4 $ genJ'
pc21r <- seek "*" $ entropy 0.6 $ genFrom pc21 3 4
pc21a <- seek "*" $ attempt 3 8 $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.4 $ genJ''

-- Play it: ordinary ProgressionContext — voicings, kinetics and
-- arrange all work unchanged on 3-6-tone chords, and the walking
-- bass reads the jazz vocabulary automatically (Section 16).
p21 = do
  let k = iK tempo [at 0 1 1 pc21] (rep pc21 1)
  mapM_ id [hush, setbpm tempo
    , (d01 $ arrange (0, 1) k (-9, 9) T flow (overlapF 0) ["~", "[0 1 2 3]/4"] # ch 01 |* vel 0.7 |= legato 1.2)
    , (p "lineHarmony" $ lineHarmony 0.9 k root ["~", "[1 2 3 4]/4"] # ch 08 # legato 0.95)
    ]

p21

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- The guided tour with live database probes is
-- live/JAZZ_DIAGNOSTIC.tidal — work down it section by section.
-- Compare pc21 and pc21s grids: the Debussy steer audibly drags
-- the walk into maj7 colour. Then mute the pad and listen to the
-- bass alone: on a 13th chord it reaches the fifth the voicing
-- never states, and it steps around the altered tones.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 22 — Snippets & the liveness gradient
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- live/snippets.cson   (the snippet library — Pulsar format)
--
-- GOAL: Choose, deliberately, how much of a performance is
-- prepared and how much is conjured at the keyboard.
--
-- The snippet library (live/snippets.cson, for Pulsar with the
-- TidalCycles grammar: type a prefix, press Tab, fill the
-- tab-stops) scaffolds every structure this guide has built.
-- Three working modes, on a gradient:
--
--   PREPARED STATE FILE — a complete launch block evaluated in
--     one gesture. Instant sound, everything decided in advance.
--     Fastest to music; least is decided live.
--
--   SNIPPET EXPANSION — structure arrives from the library,
--     decisions (key, composer, entropy, patterns) are made live
--     at the tab-stops. The skeleton is given; the choices are
--     yours, in front of the audience.
--
--   BLANK PAGE — everything typed. Slowest to music; every
--     decision is visible and live.
--
-- None is "more correct" — they trade immediacy against
-- decidedness. A performance can move along the gradient:
-- open from a state file, develop by snippet, end blank-page.
--
-- ── THE PREFIX INDEX (live/snippets.cson) ──
--   session      transport · state (a full launch block) ·
--                movement (a full suite-movement skeleton)
--   generation   lead · ctx · gen · formless · dance
--   launcher     launch · p · rrange · slate · minimal · deeptech
--   groove/lines subk · walk
--   motifs       mpanel · motif · develop
--   control      cc · disp
--   orchestra*   orchsec · orchblend · tutti · instr · divisi ·
--                divisidesk
--   hardware*    k909 · kmpc · kgrv · m32 · mdf
--   (* rig-specific — see KNOWN EXCEPTIONS)
--
-- The `state` snippet is the canonical prepared opening: tempo,
-- context, a generated progression under `attempt`, a one-node
-- form, and a launcher with commented instrument lines — the
-- same shape this guide has been building by hand since
-- Section 3. Everything you've learned here IS the snippet
-- library's content; the gradient is just how much of it you
-- re-derive in the moment.

-- ── TRY THIS ──
-- Rebuild Section 3's launcher from memory on a blank page,
-- timing yourself. Then do it from the `state` snippet. The
-- difference is the gradient, felt.

hush


-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 23 — Going further
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--
-- INSTRUMENT CATALOGUE
--   live/ORCHESTRAL_CATALOGUE.tidal  — range tests per instrument
--
-- DOCUMENTATION
--   USER_GUIDE.md                — readable version of this guide
--   CHANGELOG.md                 — V3 feature summary
--   documents/OCTATRIPENTATONICS.md        — strata/tristrata reference
--   documents/ALGORITHMIC_ORCHESTRATION.md — virtual orchestra
--   documents/ARCHITECTURE.md              — R→E→T, layers, graph schema
--
-- DRUM PATTERNS
--   live/drumpats/               — genre pattern library
--     (kick/snare/hh helpers in BootTidal.hs)
--
-- ── QUICK REFERENCE ──
--
-- Generation:
--   start <- lead "C maj"                          -- or initCadenceState
--   s <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 4 $ entropy 0.5 $ gen
--   s <- seek "bach:30 debussy:70" $ ... $ gen'    -- blend + diagnostics
--   s <- seek "none" $ ... $ gen                   -- offline fallback
--
-- Rank & select:
--   seek "*" $ attempt 3 12 $ ... $ gen            -- best of K
--   viability 0.7 $ attempt 5 24 $ ... $ gen       -- stricter floor
--
-- Regenerate in place:
--   s' <- seek "*" $ entropy 0.5 $ genFrom s 3 4   -- bars 3–4 only
--
-- Strata-first (T/S/M layers):
--   pc <- seek "*" $ cue start $ len 8 $ genVI'    -- genI..genXI ·'·''
--   hcTristrata "5" · relStrata "1 2 3" · absStrata "I V X"
--   sameBoost/flipBoost/triBoost N · genPReport (pure pc)
--
-- Context modifiers (compose with $, right-to-left):
--   hContext, hcOvertones, hcKey, hcRoots, hcPedal,
--   consonant, dissonant, invSkip, hcTristrata
--
-- Voicing functions: flow, grid, lite, root, fund
--
-- Chord selection: rep s 1, warp "[1 2 3 4]/4"
--
-- Arrangement:
--   arrange  (lo,hi) k (-9,9) LAYER voicing modifier [pats] # ch N
--   arrange' — squeeze variant · LAYER = T | S | M
--
-- Form node: at time kinetics dynamics progression   (secs, smooth)
--   at' = secs+snap · rh = bars+smooth · rh' = bars+snap
--   iK tempo [nodes] chordSel · lK kinSig dynSig s chordSel (live)
--   ki (lo,hi) k pat · slate (lo,hi) k pats · withForm k f
--
-- Groove & lines:
--   subKick d k root (maxDur, subOn, subOff, kickPat)   -- ch 10
--   lineHarmony d k root ["~", "[1 2 3 4]/4"]           -- walking bass
--
-- Motifs: (rhythm, contour, motif) = (.., .., rhythm >:< contour)
--   develop: rev, mirror axis, slow/fast, |+, <~, struct / >:<
--   claves (BootTidal): son32, rumba32, bossa32, bellpat32 (+ 23s)
--
-- Manipulation: rotate, excerpt, transposeP, reverse, fuse, fuse2,
--               interleave, expandP, insert, switch, clone, extract,
--               progOverlap, progOverlapF, progOverlapB
--
-- Explicit: fromChords [[0,4,7], ...] · prog (notesToPCs <$> [[C,E,G]])
--           fromCadenceStates [initCadenceState mov "Root" [ints], ...]
--
-- Snippets (Pulsar): transport · state · lead · ctx · gen ·
--   formless · dance · launch · p · rrange · slate · minimal ·
--   deeptech · subk · walk · mpanel · motif · develop · cc · disp

hush
